    "body": "<p>A soundscape is not a fixed object: it changes with the seasons, and listening across a year is the only way to know a place&#x27;s full acoustic life. Spring brings migrant songbirds and breeding choruses, summer brings insects and fledgling begging calls, autumn brings departing migrants and rutting deer, and winter brings wind, ice, and the calls of resident species that stay. Geophony follows its own seasons: spring meltwater, summer thunderstorms, autumn gales, and winter ice. Anthrophony has seasons too: harvest machinery in autumn, snowploughs in winter, tourism in summer. Long-term recordings capture all of this, and ecologists use seasonal soundscape patterns, phenology, to track climate change, since earlier springs shift the arrival of song and chorus by measurable days. This record covers all three layers, because seasonal change touches each of them.</p><h4>Science proof</h4><p>Science proof: long-term acoustic monitoring programmes document seasonal phenology in bird song, frog choruses, and insect activity, and show shifts in timing correlated with temperature trends. Seasonal soundscape indices computed month by month reveal the annual cycle and its anomalies, and those anomalies are published alongside temperature and migration records for cross-checking.</p><h4>Math proof</h4><p>Math proof: if spring chorus onset advances by 2 days per decade, then over 5 decades it advances by 2 x 5 = 10 days. The demo prints advance per decade 2 days, decades 5, total advance 10 days, and the new onset day if the old onset was day 100 of the year, day 90.</p><h4>Soundscape layer(s)</h4><p>biophony; geophony; anthrophony</p><h4>Source</h4><p>Published acoustic phenology and climate-change soundscape literature; figures as published October 2026</p>",
